from mk import mk
mk("leLAm3OigI0","How to Design Cinematic Realistic String Pads - Zebra 3 Sound Design Tutorial",
 "ensemble-string-pad-dual-noise-comb-lanes-random-drift-keytracked-darkening-velocity-env-bow-tilt-lfos",
 "Cinematic ensemble string pad from noise-excited combs: two comb lanes (static vs pink noise, different comb flavours) with fast random pitch drift for a section feel, key-tracked low-pass so low notes stop sounding metallic, a smoothed saw layer and an EQ'd sine/FM low end, a low-boosting resonator, mod-wheel cutoff, velocity that shortens attack and release, a tiny strike pitch envelope and offset triangle 'bow' LFOs on comb tilt",
 "Build a realistic, wide sustained string pad (not a solo bowed voice) on comb filters. Lane A: static noise with a slow attack and slow release into a comb whose feedback is raised until sharp, low-passed to take the edge off, with a fast random (non-synced) ramp LFO on fine detune so it behaves like many players. Lane B: pink noise (more warmth and body) into a different comb flavour, also detuned. Mix both lanes and control them through one filter with cutoff key-tracked (centre near middle E, about 64 in the middle) so higher keys stay bright and low keys are darker and less metallic; add a little comb distortion. Add body with a smoothed saw (curve filter in string range, doubled for width) and a sine/FM layer EQ'd to low end only. Global FX: subtle mod/diffusion (slow shape), reverb, an EQ resonator mode boosting lows on lane 2, light peak compression. Make it playable: mod wheel → cutoff, velocity → shorter attack and shorter release (never zero), a tiny curved pitch rise on strike applied to both comb pitches, and triangle LFOs at different second-based rates on comb tilt/flavour (not 'structure', which changes pitch). Distinct from today's MPE bowed-string skill: an ensemble pad with drift, dual-lane flavours and velocity/LFO articulation.",
 ["Synth with comb filters fed by noise (static/white and pink noise types), LFOs, key tracking and global FX (Zebra 3 used in the source), or Web Audio: two noise sources into per-voice tuned feedback delay combs (implementation note)","Mod wheel and velocity-sensitive keyboard","Reverb, mod/diffusion effect, EQ with resonator mode or a resonator, compressor"],
 [("chords","midi","Pad chords to play",True),("comb_flavours","list","Two different comb tone/flavour settings for lanes A and B",True),("drift_rate","number","Random detune LFO speed (faster than a slow drift, short of a vibrato)",False)],
 [("string_pad","patch","Wide, organic cinematic string pad"),("articulation_map","table","Mod wheel, velocity, strike pitch and bow LFO routings")],
 ["Spoken values only (key-track centre near middle E / ~64, comb feedback 'until sharp'). Other amounts are explicitly 'to taste'.","Keep the random detune subtle; too much 'sounds awful'.","Mod/diffusion effects can add audible static noise; use little. Heavy compression lifts any noise floor.","Do not modulate the comb 'structure' with the bow LFO; it changes pitch. Use tilt/flavour instead."],
 [("Lane A: static noise → amp envelope with slow attack and slow release → comb. Raise comb feedback until the tone is sharp and pitched; low-pass the noise to remove edge.","lane A","Pitched, slightly harsh comb pad"),
  ("Add an LFO set to random ramp, not tempo-synced, routed lightly to fine detune; speed it up until it reads as a group of players, stopping short of vibrato.","ensemble drift","Natural section-like pitch variation"),
  ("Global FX: a little mod/diffusion (slow shape), reverb; set a non-resonant 6 dB linear filter as a starting point and increase width.","space","Sound takes on string quality and widens"),
  ("Lane B: duplicate the idea with pink noise into a different comb flavour/complexity, detuned the same way. Mix lanes A and B and route both through one shared filter.","lane B","Thicker, warmer pad; the two lanes differ slightly for intensity"),
  ("Key-track the shared filter cutoff with the centre near middle E (about 64 in the middle). Add a little comb distortion if wanted.","keytrack","High notes stay bright; low notes no longer sound metallic"),
  ("Body: add a saw oscillator smoothed with a curve filter in string range (double it for width) and a sine/FM layer EQ'd to keep only low end. Optionally add an EQ resonator boosting lows on lane 2 in the global FX.","body","Full low end and width"),
  ("Playability: mod wheel → filter cutoff; velocity → attack and release so harder strikes get shorter attack and shorter release (keep a minimum above zero); compress only the top peaks.","expression","Dynamic, controllable pad"),
  ("Strike pitch: a short curved envelope adding a tiny pitch rise to both comb pitches (exaggerate to hear, then reduce). Bow motion: triangle LFOs with rates in seconds on comb tilt/flavour, at different rates per lane so they do not move in unison.","articulation","Subtle bow-like movement and attack realism")],
 ["Low notes sound noticeably darker than high notes at the same settings (key-tracked cutoff).","Hard velocity gives a faster, shorter note than soft velocity.","Mod wheel audibly opens the filter.","The two lanes' tilt LFOs are out of phase (differing rates) on a scope or by ear."],
 ["string-pad","comb-filter","noise-exciter","ensemble","key-tracking","velocity","cinematic","zebra"],
 note="route.py said COVERED; hand-checked as a false positive. Distinct from the MPE bowed-string skill.")

mk("Q_0QcuRePZ0","How to Create Epic Sound FX and Risers That Transform Your Tracks | 100 Sound Design Tips Ep. 13",
 "one-bar-transition-riser-mod-envelope-timeline-atomized-targets-triangle-sync-ducked-reverb-tail",
 "One-bar transition FX built on a single mod-envelope 'timeline': a rising curve with an end drop drives noise level, full left-to-right pan and a resonant driven filter; an octave-down detuned unison saw on its own amp; a second up-and-down triangle envelope on oscillator sync (~50%); multiband mid distortion; master delay then a sidechain-ducked reverb that blooms after the riser cuts",
 "Design reusable section-transition risers in any synth with drawable envelopes. Choose the length first (usually one bar; half or two bars as needed) and draw a mod envelope from bottom at 0 to top at the bar end, then a steep drop to zero as a safeguard. Use it to raise noise from silence (depth sets loudness), pan from fully left to fully right (set pan hard left, modulate 100%), and open a bright filter with resonance and drive. Add a second voice: a saw an octave down with unison and detune on its own amp and FX bus so each target is controlled separately ('atomized'). Draw a second envelope as a triangle (up then down within the same bar) for oscillator sync (~50% depth). Modulate a multiband distortion's mid band with the timeline. Put tails on the master after distortion: delay with lows and highs cut, then reverb with decay below ~70% and sidechain ducking so the reverb opens up as the riser ends. Optional: stutter/glitch FX before the reverb, a master filter sweep, curve variants and bar-length macros. Distinct from our pitch-ramp and Shepard risers: a multi-target envelope timeline with a ducked tail.",
 ["Synth with drawable multi-point mod envelopes, multiple amps/FX buses, noise and saw oscillators with unison and sync, multiband distortion, delay, reverb with sidechain (Avenger in the source), or Web Audio AudioParam setValueCurveAtTime timelines (implementation note)","A track section boundary to place the transition on"],
 [("length_bars","number","Transition length; usually 1 bar (or 0.5, 2)",True),("curve_shape","enum","Linear, exponential-in, slow-drop or spiky variants",False),("targets","list","Noise level, pan, filter cutoff, sync, distortion, master filter",True)],
 [("transition_fx","audio","Riser/transition that bridges two song sections"),("patch","patch","Reusable transition patch with swappable envelope curves")],
 ["Spoken values: one bar in most cases, pan modulated 100% from hard left, sync ~50%, reverb decay below ~70%. Other depths are by ear.","Modulate per-target levels (separate amps) rather than a master amp, otherwise everything on that amp moves together.","Put delay/reverb after distortion on the master so tails are not distorted; a send would place the reverb before the multiband distortion.","Add a little release to avoid clicks at the end drop."],
 [("Pick the length (usually one bar). Draw mod envelope 1: start at the bottom at 0, rise to the top at the bar end (4 beats), then add a steep drop back to 0.","timeline","A rising control curve with a guaranteed end"),
  ("Noise: oscillator gain at 0, noise level 0, mod envelope 1 → noise level with depth for loudness. Bend the curve for more impact; add a compressor or amp gain if it is too quiet.","noise rise","Noise swells across the bar and cuts"),
  ("Pan: set noise pan hard left and modulate it 100% with the same envelope (unipolar), so it travels left to right.","pan","Noise sweeps across the stereo field"),
  ("Filter: bright filter with some resonance and drive, cutoff modulated by the same envelope; add a little release.","filter","Resonant opening sweep, no click"),
  ("Second voice: a saw an octave down with unison and detune on a separate amp and FX bus. Its amp level starts at 0 and is modulated by envelope 1.","octave voice","Detuned body rising under the noise"),
  ("Draw mod envelope 2 as a triangle over the same bar (up to the top then back down) and route it to oscillator sync (~50%). Try curve variants (fast-in or slow-drop before the downbeat).","sync","Sync sweep that peaks and returns within the bar"),
  ("On the second voice's FX bus, modulate a multiband distortion's mid drive with envelope 1.","distortion","Growing grit toward the transition"),
  ("Master tails after distortion: delay (lows and highs cut, more mix), then reverb with decay below ~70% and sidechain ducking so it stays down while the riser plays and blooms after it cuts. Optional: stutter FX between delay and reverb, a master filter sweep on envelope 1, bar-length macros (4/2/½ bars). Place a low impact/kick on the downbeat.","tails","Transition carries into the next section with a washing reverb tail")],
 ["The riser level is zero at bar start and ends exactly at the drop point.","A stereo meter shows the noise moving from left to right over the bar.","The sync sweep peaks mid-bar and returns by the end (triangle envelope).","The reverb tail is louder after the riser stops than during it (ducking works)."],
 ["riser","transition","sound-fx","mod-envelope","automation","sidechain-reverb","oscillator-sync","cinematic"],
 note="Synth-specific envelope UI mapped to a generic multi-point envelope timeline.")

mk("mllvToyambI","Magic Blur - Overview and Tutorial",
 "spectral-frame-blur-freeze-pad-polyrhythmic-blur-rates-quantized-pitch-lush-mode-sidechain-wet",
 "Spectral blur/freeze pad: capture incoming audio as spectral frames and morph frame-to-frame at tempo-synced blur rates (quarter notes, plus simultaneous triplet/dotted rates for polyrhythms) with a per-frame attack/decay envelope, pitch the blurred signal ±24 st (semitone-quantised), choose clicky vs lush modes, play frames forward/backward, freeze completely, and modulate with curves, cross-modulation and a kick-sidechained follower on dry/wet",
 "Turn any input into lush pads or frozen textures with a spectral frame effect. The input is analysed into momentary spectral snapshots (frames, like video frames). Instead of playing them in order, the effect morphs from one frame to the next at a blur rate (e.g. one frame per quarter note); several blur rates can run at once, including triplets and dotted values, for blur polyrhythms. A per-playback envelope (attack/decay) makes the stepping audible or smooth. Pitch the blurred signal up/down to 24 semitones, quantised to exact semitones when needed. Pick an algorithm flavour: the default/rhythmic sound is harsh and clicky-metallic; lush/melodic washes it out. Set playback direction forward/backward and use freeze to stop morphing entirely. Modulate the envelope and pitch with drawn curves (invert/shift/skew/morph between curves), cross-modulate the first layer's LFO rate, and use a sidechained envelope follower (kick) to move dry/wet and curve amounts. Partly fills our open spectral (FFT) freeze-pad gap.",
 ["Spectral blur/freeze effect, or Web Audio AudioWorklet STFT: capture FFT magnitude frames, interpolate between frames at the blur rate, resynthesise with randomised or held phase (implementation note)","Tempo source for blur rates; envelope follower with sidechain input"],
 [("source","audio","Input audio (vocal, pad, loop)",True),("blur_rates","list","Note values for frame morphing, e.g. 1/4, 1/4T, 1/8.",True),("pitch_st","number","Pitch shift of the blurred signal, −24…+24",False),("mode","enum","rhythmic (clicky) | melodic/lush | mixed",False)],
 [("blurred_texture","audio","Lush pad or frozen texture derived from the input"),("mod_setup","table","Curves, cross-mod and sidechain follower routings")],
 ["Spoken behaviour only: blur rate per note value, pitch ±24 st, quantised semitone toggle, rhythmic/melodic/mixed/lush modes, forward/backward, freeze. No numeric envelope times are given.","Default algorithm is harsh and clicky-metallic; use lush or melodic mode or a longer per-frame envelope for pads.","Product overview; affiliate/website CTAs dropped."],
 [("Insert the spectral blur on a source and set one blur rate (a quarter note: one frame morph per beat).","blur","Input becomes a stepping spectral smear"),
  ("Adjust the per-playback envelope (attack/decay) to hear or hide the frame steps.","envelope","Choppy stepped frames vs smooth morphing"),
  ("Add simultaneous blur rates including triplets and dotted notes for polyrhythmic blurring.","polyrhythm","Layered, shifting rhythmic smears"),
  ("Pitch the blurred signal up or down (to ±24 st), enabling semitone quantisation for musical intervals.","pitch","Octave/fifth-shifted spectral pad"),
  ("Choose the flavour: rhythmic (clicky metallic), melodic or lush (washed out), or mixed; set playback direction forward/backward.","mode","Pad-suitable lush texture or metallic rhythmic texture"),
  ("Enable freeze to stop the frame morphing and sustain the captured spectrum.","freeze","A held, frozen spectral pad"),
  ("Modulate envelope and pitch with drawn curves (randomise, invert, shift, skew, morph between up to four curves); cross-modulate the first layer's LFO rate; add an envelope follower in sidechain mode from the kick to move dry/wet and curve amounts.","modulation","Evolving soundscape that breathes with the drums")],
 ["With freeze on, the output stays sustained after the input stops.","Changing the blur rate from 1/4 to 1/8 doubles the frame-step rate.","With semitone quantisation on, the pitched output lands on exact semitone intervals (tuner check).","Kick hits audibly change the dry/wet balance when the sidechain follower is engaged."],
 ["spectral","freeze","blur","fft","pad","polyrhythm","pitch-shift","sidechain"],
 note="Short product overview; procedure is spoken behaviour, not on-screen values.")
